---
// Contributing: a summary of the repository's CONTRIBUTING.md, which stays
// the source of truth (the site reads only docs/, so this page links to it).
import DocsLayout from "../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import Command from "../../components/Command.astro";
import { CHECKS, DESKTOP } from "../../data/build";
import { SITE, repoFile, href } from "../../lib/site";

const headings = [
  { depth: 2, id: "ways-to-help", text: "Ways to help" },
  { depth: 2, id: "before-changing-code", text: "Before changing code" },
  { depth: 2, id: "develop-and-check", text: "Develop and check" },
  { depth: 2, id: "send-a-change", text: "Send a change" },
  { depth: 2, id: "ai-assisted-contributions", text: "AI-assisted contributions" },
  { depth: 2, id: "license", text: "License" },
];
---
<DocsLayout
  title="Contributing"
  description="How to report a bug, improve the documentation or send a change to OpenFrame, with the checks every change runs and the rules for AI-assisted work."
  slug="contributing"
  headings={headings}
>
  <p>
    OpenFrame is built in the open at <a href={SITE.repo}>{SITE.repoName}</a>, and issues and pull requests there are the way
    in. This page summarizes the repository's <a href={repoFile("CONTRIBUTING.md")}>CONTRIBUTING.md</a>; when they differ,
    that file wins.
  </p>

  <h2 id="ways-to-help">Ways to help</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Report a bug you can reproduce</strong>, with the app revision, your OS, desktop or browser, the steps, what you expected and what happened. <a href={`${SITE.repo}/issues`}>Open an issue</a>.</li>
    <li><strong>Describe a usability problem</strong> in terms of the editing task you were doing; a small screenshot or sample project helps.</li>
    <li><strong>Improve</strong> documentation, accessibility, keyboard workflows, format support, tests or existing features. Read the <a href={repoFile("docs/Roadmap.md")}>roadmap</a> before a large change.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Use media you can share. Remove credentials, MCP tokens, private footage and local paths from reports. For
    security-sensitive reports, contact the owner privately while a public reporting channel is being prepared.
  </p>

  <h2 id="before-changing-code">Before changing code</h2>
  <p>
    Read <a href={repoFile("AGENTS.md")}>AGENTS.md</a>, <a href={repoFile("CLAUDE.md")}>CLAUDE.md</a>,
    the <a href={repoFile("docs/Conventions.md")}>conventions</a>, the <a href={href("docs/architecture/")}>architecture</a>
    and <a href={repoFile("docs/Next agent handoff.md")}>the handoff</a>; the
    <a href={repoFile("docs/Index.md")}>documentation index</a> links every decision and research note. Look at
    <code>git status</code>, recent history and existing worktrees, and coordinate with work already under way.
  </p>
  <p>
    Features land on desktop first. Keep platform differences behind <code>src/platform/</code>, and do not add a server or
    an upload step as an incidental choice: OpenFrame runs on the user's hardware.
  </p>

  <h2 id="develop-and-check">Develop and check</h2>
  <p>
    The editor is in <code>openframe-application/</code> and the site in <code>openframe-website/</code>; <code>docs/</code> at the root
    serves both. Run every command below in <code>openframe-application/</code>.
  </p>
  <Command label="Run the desktop app" code={DESKTOP} />
  <p>Before proposing a change, run the checks (<code>just check</code> runs them all). CI runs the same gates when the maintainer starts it, so run them yourself:</p>
  <Command label="The checks" code={CHECKS} />
  <p>
    Then try the change in the running product, not only in tests. Desktop changes need desktop checks: a browser drive does
    not prove native dialogs, paths, FFmpeg or the release security policy. <a href={repoFile("docs/Driving the app.md")}>Driving the app</a>
    explains how agents and scripts drive it.
  </p>

  <h2 id="send-a-change">Send a change</h2>
  <p>
    Keep it focused, and explain the problem, the result, the tradeoffs and how you checked it. Update the feature docs for
    shipped behavior, the roadmap for plans, an architecture decision record for a non-obvious choice, and the history for
    lessons the next contributor should not have to learn again.
  </p>

  <h2 id="ai-assisted-contributions">AI-assisted contributions</h2>
  <p>
    Claude Code and Codex were the first development tools, and other tools are welcome. Say when AI helped substantially,
    and keep the useful reasoning and evidence in the docs, not private prompts or raw session logs. You are responsible
    for understanding the change and for real evidence: never report an unrun test or a guessed result as a pass.
  </p>

  <h2 id="license">License</h2>
  <p>
    Contributions are offered under <a href={SITE.licenseUrl}>GPL-3.0-only</a>, the project's license. Submit only work you
    are entitled to contribute, keep existing notices, and explain a new dependency's purpose and license before adding it.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
